1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,516
Don't open the box.

2
00:00:03,816 --> 00:00:08,677
Pandora's box is one of the best-known
images from Greek myth.

3
00:00:08,977 --> 00:00:12,826
A woman is given a box, she
is told never to open it,

4
00:00:12,826 --> 00:00:16,527
and when she does, every
evil in the world escapes.

5
00:00:16,827 --> 00:00:20,248
But in the oldest source
we have, there is no box.

6
00:00:20,548 --> 00:00:24,689
And the story it tells is stranger
than the one we remember.

7
00:00:28,767 --> 00:00:34,298
In this episode, we follow Pandora
from a Greek poet of around 700 BCE,

8
00:00:34,298 --> 00:00:40,066
through ancient storage jars and a later
fable, to a Renaissance scholar's

9
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:45,597
book of proverbs and the paintings
that gave her the box we know today.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,825
The earliest account comes from Hesiod,

11
00:00:53,825 --> 00:00:57,230
the same poet who described
the birth of Medusa.

12
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:00,998
In two of his poems,
the Theogony and the Works

13
00:01:00,998 --> 00:01:04,164
and Days, Pandora's
story begins with fire.

14
00:01:04,464 --> 00:01:09,103
Prometheus has stolen fire
from Zeus and given it to humans.

15
00:01:09,403 --> 00:01:12,093
Zeus answers with a gift of his own.

16
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,728
At his command, the smith god Hephaestus
shapes a young woman from earth and water.

17
00:01:19,028 --> 00:01:22,005
Athena teaches her weaving
and dresses her.

18
00:01:22,305 --> 00:01:25,580
Aphrodite gives her grace,
and painful longing.

19
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:32,281
And Hermes gives her, in Hesiod's words,
a shameless mind and a deceitful nature.

20
00:01:32,581 --> 00:01:36,035
Because all who lived
on Olympus gave her a gift,

21
00:01:36,035 --> 00:01:38,842
she is named Pandora, the "all-gifted."

22
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:46,417
In the Theogony, she is not even named.

23
00:01:46,717 --> 00:01:51,370
Hesiod calls her a beautiful
evil, a trap set for humankind,

24
00:01:51,370 --> 00:01:54,683
and presents her as the first
of all women.

25
00:01:54,983 --> 00:01:57,875
His poems are openly hostile to women.

26
00:01:58,175 --> 00:02:00,997
That view belongs to Hesiod and his time,

27
00:02:00,997 --> 00:02:04,714
and it is one more reason
to read the source carefully.

28
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:11,069
In the Works and Days, Hesiod
tells what happened next.

29
00:02:11,369 --> 00:02:13,942
Prometheus had warned his brother

30
00:02:13,942 --> 00:02:17,296
Epimetheus never to accept
a gift from Zeus.

31
00:02:17,596 --> 00:02:19,848
But Epimetheus accepted her.

32
00:02:20,148 --> 00:02:25,690
Before this, people had lived free from
evils, from hard work, and from sickness.

33
00:02:25,990 --> 00:02:30,553
Then she lifted the great lid of a jar
and scattered what was inside.

34
00:02:30,853 --> 00:02:33,789
Countless troubles spread among humankind.

35
00:02:37,667 --> 00:02:44,841
The Greek word Hesiod uses is pithos, a
large storage jar for grain, oil, or wine.

36
00:02:45,141 --> 00:02:49,259
This one, made on Crete
around 675 BCE and now

37
00:02:49,259 --> 00:02:53,833
in the Louvre, is about
one and a half meters tall.

38
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:57,301
Hesiod does not say
where the jar came from.

39
00:02:57,601 --> 00:03:00,393
And the only warning in his poem is about

40
00:03:00,393 --> 00:03:03,457
accepting the gift, not
about opening the jar.

41
00:03:06,100 --> 00:03:08,309
Only one thing stayed inside.

42
00:03:08,609 --> 00:03:11,342
Hope remained beneath the rim of the jar.

43
00:03:11,642 --> 00:03:15,810
Before it could fly out, the lid
stopped it, by the will of Zeus.

44
00:03:17,067 --> 00:03:18,467
Why hope?

45
00:03:18,527 --> 00:03:21,847
Readers have argued about
this line for centuries.

46
00:03:22,147 --> 00:03:25,527
Is hope kept safe for us,
or kept away from us?

47
00:03:25,827 --> 00:03:31,619
The Greek word, elpis, can mean hope,
but also expectation of what is to come.

48
00:03:31,919 --> 00:03:34,012
Hesiod does not explain.

49
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:43,648
Greek artists also told
the story of Pandora's making.

50
00:03:43,948 --> 00:03:49,196
The ancient traveler Pausanias wrote
that her birth was shown in relief

51
00:03:49,196 --> 00:03:53,843
on the base of the great statue
of Athena inside the Parthenon.

52
00:03:54,143 --> 00:03:56,341
The statue itself is lost.

53
00:03:56,641 --> 00:03:59,597
But this small copy from Roman times still

54
00:03:59,597 --> 00:04:02,623
carries a version
of that scene on its base.

55
00:04:04,067 --> 00:04:06,882
And the jar did not always hold evils.

56
00:04:07,182 --> 00:04:11,121
A later Greek fable, collected
under the name of Babrius,

57
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:14,497
tells of a jar that Zeus
filled with good things.

58
00:04:14,797 --> 00:04:17,607
A man who could not resist lifted the lid,

59
00:04:17,607 --> 00:04:20,416
and the good things
flew up to the heavens.

60
00:04:20,716 --> 00:04:22,893
Only hope was left behind.

61
00:04:23,193 --> 00:04:26,382
In this fable, Pandora is not even named.

62
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:30,156
So where did the box come from?

63
00:04:30,456 --> 00:04:34,601
The change is usually
traced to the early 1500s,

64
00:04:34,601 --> 00:04:38,305
more than two thousand years after Hesiod.

65
00:04:38,605 --> 00:04:42,449
The Dutch scholar Erasmus
retold the story in Latin,

66
00:04:42,449 --> 00:04:46,067
in his famous collection
of proverbs, the Adages.

67
00:04:46,367 --> 00:04:51,959
In this edition, printed
in Venice in 1508, where Hesiod

68
00:04:51,959 --> 00:04:57,754
had pithos, a great jar, Erasmus
wrote pyxis, a small box.

69
00:04:58,054 --> 00:05:03,293
And in his retelling, it is Epimetheus,
not Pandora, who opens it.

70
00:05:04,200 --> 00:05:05,600
Why a box?

71
00:05:05,861 --> 00:05:09,190
Scholars have suggested
that Erasmus may have been

72
00:05:09,190 --> 00:05:12,655
thinking of another story,
the Roman tale of Psyche.

73
00:05:12,955 --> 00:05:15,863
She is sent to the underworld
to bring back

74
00:05:15,863 --> 00:05:18,980
a small box, and is warned
not to look inside.

75
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:21,569
On the way back, she opens it.

76
00:05:21,869 --> 00:05:24,343
Whatever the reason, the word stuck.

77
00:05:28,767 --> 00:05:31,647
Erasmus's book was read across Europe.

78
00:05:31,947 --> 00:05:38,555
Later artists gave Pandora many kinds of
containers: caskets, chests, and vessels.

79
00:05:38,855 --> 00:05:44,355
Some still showed a great jar,
like this print from the 1500s.

80
00:05:44,655 --> 00:05:48,204
But over time, the box
became the familiar image,

81
00:05:48,204 --> 00:05:52,344
and "Pandora's box" became
an everyday phrase in English,

82
00:05:52,344 --> 00:05:56,559
for something that brings many
troubles once it is opened.

83
00:05:58,367 --> 00:06:01,033
So was Pandora's box ever a box?

84
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:04,565
In Hesiod, it is a great storage jar.

85
00:06:04,865 --> 00:06:08,138
In a later fable, the jar
holds good things.

86
00:06:08,438 --> 00:06:12,733
In a Renaissance book of proverbs,
it becomes a small box.

87
00:06:13,033 --> 00:06:16,840
And in paintings, it
is whatever the artist imagined.

88
00:06:18,933 --> 00:06:22,646
The box we know is not
the oldest part of the story.

89
00:06:22,946 --> 00:06:24,847
It is one of the newest.

90
00:06:25,147 --> 00:06:27,474
Every story has an origin.

91
00:06:27,774 --> 00:06:30,749
Sometimes, even the objects in it do.

92
00:06:33,567 --> 00:06:38,396
This video is based on ancient
texts and public museum collections.

93
00:06:38,696 --> 00:06:41,653
The narration uses synthetic speech,

94
00:06:41,653 --> 00:06:45,595
and some images are
AI-generated reconstructions.

95
00:06:45,895 --> 00:06:49,841
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

96
00:06:50,141 --> 00:06:52,909
If you enjoyed this story, please like

97
00:06:52,909 --> 00:06:56,261
the video and subscribe
for more quiet origins.

98
00:06:56,561 --> 00:06:58,334
Thank you for watching.
